{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 47, :comments ["<b>The garment, etc.</b> The saying of the conjunctive vav — Rabbi Yosei HaGelili derived it (Torat Kohanim), and this is his language: ״Outside the camp is his dwelling and the garment״ — this teaches concerning garments that they require banishment outside the three camps. And they further said there: I have only wool and linen that are specified; from where do I include mixed fabric (kilayim)? Scripture says to say ״וְהַבֶּגֶד״ etc. And it is difficult: perhaps the inclusive vav of ״וְהַבֶּגֶד״ comes only either to attach it to the law of banishment outside the three camps or to include kilayim. Perhaps, however, the derashah of Rabbi Yosei HaGelili is from the vav, and the derashah of including kilayim is from the heh of ״וְהַבֶּגֶד,״ from the fact that it did not say ״וּבֶגֶד.״" "<b>In a woolen garment or in a garment, etc.</b> One must know why it said ״or״ and was not satisfied to say ״in a woolen garment and in a linen garment,״ and all the more so according to the words of the Rav, the author of Korban Aharon, who explained that the reason it was necessary to include kilayim is because Scripture said ״or,״ which separates each one on its own — the difficulty grows great: there was no need to write neither ״or״ nor the inclusion of ״וְהַבֶּגֶד.״ And the difficulty grows even greater from what they said there in Torat Kohanim: One might think they become impurity-affected whether dyed or not dyed; Scripture says to say ״in a woolen garment or in a linen garment״ — just as linen is in its natural state, so too wool is in its natural state; thus far. It is difficult: does not Scripture, on the contrary, contradict this by saying ״or,״ which separated them, to say that one should not learn wool from linen." "<b>And it appears</b> that Scripture needed to say ״or,״ lest a person err and say that a garment does not become impurity-affected through nega'im unless it is a garment of both of them together in this manner — a woolen garment and a linen garment with it; Scripture says ״or.״ If so, the word ״or״ is needed for itself and does not preclude drawing an association between wool and linen, and this is the sense of the verse: ״in a woolen garment,״ and not specifically wool but the same law applies if it is a linen garment — both are equal in law. And therefore they derived ״just as linen is in its natural state״ — meaning that it is not the way of people to dye linen garments, and likewise wool, etc. And the reason it was necessary to include kilayim from ״וְהַבֶּגֶד,״ is not because of the error that would arise from the word ״or״ as the author of Korban Aharon thought, but because Scripture stated only wool and linen — from where do we derive kilayim? And if we were to say that it was necessary because it said ״or,״ according to the words of the author of Korban Aharon, if so the word ״or״ would carry the implication that it severed between wool and linen for all matters — for this reason Scripture needed to include kilayim, and for this reason Scripture needed ״or״ because it severed. From this point there would be no place to say ״just as linen is in its natural state,״ for ״or״ severed between them — and understand."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:56:22.959879627Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}